The generalized law of refraction in 3-D is given by the equations:
\[
\begin{cases}
n_t \sin(\theta_t) - n_i \sin(\theta_i) = \frac{1}{k_o} \frac{d\Phi}{dx} \\
\cos(\theta_t) \sin(\phi_t) = \frac{1}{n_t k_o} \frac{d\Phi}{dy}
\end{cases}
\]
where \( n_t \) and \( n_i \) are the refractive indices of the transmitted and incident media, respectively; \( \theta_t \) and \( \theta_i \) are the angles of refraction and incidence, respectively; \( \phi_t \) is the out-of-plane deflection angle; \( k_o \) is the wavevector in vacuum; and \( \frac{d\Phi}{dx} \) and \( \frac{d\Phi}{dy} \) are the phase gradients along the x- and y-directions, respectively.
